        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Adaptation to microgravity causes changes in the autonomic nervous system and these changes have a significant effect on the control of blood flow and blood pressure.  They result in orthostatic intolerance, meaning the ability to provide sufficient blood flow to body tissues, particularly the brain.
<P>
The investigators tested the hypothesis that adaptation to this unique environment causes alterations in the autonomic nervous system that interact with microgravity induced changes in body fluid distributio and thus result in orthostatic intolerance.  The adaptation occurs rapidly within the first few days/weeks of flight and does not progress with long term (months) of exposure.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Crewmembers were instrumented for ECG, impedance respiration, continuous finger blood pressure (Portapres), cuff pressure, and cerebral blood flow velocity (transcranial Doppler).  Maximum hand grip contraction force was also determined.  Cardiovascular monitoring was performed on the GASMAP by measuring inspired and expired tidal volume, CO2 and O2, and Valsalva maneuver. A cold pressor test was also performed, and LBNP with norepinephrine spillover was done at -15 and -30mmHg.  Following this protocol, the crewmember was Holter monitored for 24 hours.  Baroreflex was planned to be measured but wasn't due to equipment issues.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Due to the logistics of performing research on the Mir station during 2 missions with problems, the data were limited.  The team did obtain the first data extant on the effects of extreme long duration spaceflight and found that vagal cardiac control and baroreflex gain were decreased.  The team attempted to document the time course of adaptation but was unable to successfully record directional trends.  It could not be determined whether the autonomic regulation changes progressed or stabilized as a function of flight duration.  For more information on results and publications, see experiment ID E709.
        </div>

